From 05d454703a55774871694cf4edfbb05bade028f1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 04:26:09 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 17/25] NativeLayerWayland: decide through libstereo-policy

How a video holds its views is now decided by libstereo-policy
(stereo-policy.h, stereo-layer 1.4.0), the stereo policy every program
of Sparky Stereo OS shares, in plain C11 that links nothing, loaded at
run time (dlopen of libstereo-policy.so.1). The browser decides exactly
as Dolphin, the thumbnailers and the players do, and keeps no copy of
the rules.

stereo_policy_video_layout() takes the picture's own mark, which the
browser still reads (the frame packing SEI, the container's mark), as
its Stereo3DLayout word (sideBySideLeftFirst, topBottomRightFirst, and
the other packings of RFC 9559 Table 5 and H.264/H.265 Table D-8); then
the user's choice in the 3D Video menu; then the frame's shape (at least
three times as wide as high is side by side). The user's Swap Eyes is
its swap_eyes argument, over whichever decided. The user's "Not 3D"
choice goes in as a "mono" mark, which holds no packing.
stereo_policy_video_full_views() says whether the views of a mark
without the size of a view are full (side by side at a display aspect of
2.5 or more, top and bottom at 1.2 or less) or half; the browser's own
copy of that rule is gone.

libstereo-layer, which loads Qt, is no longer loaded into the browser.
Without libstereo-policy a mark is shown as it is, with half views
unless their size is given, and a video without a mark stays flat.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 via Claude Code
